# Security headers for print-tracker — Doctrine §16.6
#
# THIS FILE IS EXERCISED BY THE GATE, NOT JUST SHIPPED. tools/serve.mjs parses
# the /* block below and sends the same headers, and tools/a11y.mjs boots the app
# through it and fails on any page error — so a Content-Security-Policy that
# breaks the app goes red here rather than on a reader's device. A CSP written
# and never run is a guess with a header's authority.
#
# THIS APP CAN HAVE A REAL CSP, and that is worth stating plainly because the hub
# deliberately cannot: its content model lives in an inline <script>, so
# script-src 'self' would reduce it to its <noscript> fallback. Everything here
# is in module files and an external stylesheet, so the strict policy costs
# nothing.
#
# style-src is 'self' with NO 'unsafe-inline'. That blocks parsed style=""
# attributes, which is why public/app/dom.js applies styles through the CSSOM
# instead — assigning element.style.width is not blocked, and a style string
# passed to setAttribute would be silently dropped.
#
# connect-src IS 'self', NOT 'none', AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING. The app itself
# makes no network requests — nothing is fetched and nothing is sent. But these
# headers are served for every path under /*, INCLUDING sw.js, and a service
# worker inherits the policy delivered with its own script. Under 'none' the
# worker's cache.add() and fetch() are both blocked, so it installs, reports
# itself active, caches nothing, and the app never works offline — with no error
# anywhere a reader or a deploy log would see it. Found by tools/serve.mjs sending
# these real headers to the gate rather than letting it run without a policy.
#
# So: the only network requests this app ever makes are the service worker
# collecting this app's own files, from this origin, so it works offline.
#
# img-src CARRIES `blob:` AND THAT IS THE WHOLE PICTURE FEATURE. Pictures the
# reader adds are stored as Blobs in IndexedDB and shown through
# URL.createObjectURL, which produces a blob: URL. Under `img-src 'self'` alone
# every one of them is refused and the tiles are empty, with the reason only in
# the console.
#
# It does NOT carry `https:` or a remote host, and that is the other half: no
# picture is ever loaded from somebody else's server. A hotlinked image would
# announce to that site every time a board is drawn, break when their CDN moved,
# and vanish offline. Pictures are copied in, downscaled, and served from here.
#
# No Strict-Transport-Security: pages.dev is HTTPS-only and Cloudflare handles
# TLS for it. A header with lock-in semantics that cannot be tested from here is
# worse than leaving it to the platform.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self' blob:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; manifest-src 'self'; worker-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: accelerometer=(), camera=(), geolocation=(), gyroscope=(), magnetometer=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=(), interest-cohort=()

# ONE PAGE, AND IT IS NOT THE APP. /probe.html exists to answer a question the app
# cannot answer about itself: given a picture's address, can this browser actually
# READ the bytes, or only display them? That is a fact about the other site's CORS
# headers, and the only way to learn it is to try it on a real machine against a
# real address.
#
# It cannot be asked under the policy above — `img-src 'self' blob:` and
# `connect-src 'self'` refuse the request before any code runs, so every answer
# would be "no" and every "no" would be about US. So this block, and ONLY these
# two directives, are widened here.
#
# WRITTEN BELOW `/*` DELIBERATELY. Both blocks match this path and both set the
# same header; the later one wins, on Cloudflare Pages and in tools/serve.mjs,
# which merges in file order for exactly this reason.
#
# Everything else stays: script-src and style-src are still 'self', so the page
# runs its own external file and nothing injected, and `default-src 'self'` still
# governs every directive not named here. The page reads and writes nothing —
# it does not touch the app's database, and it stores no result.
#
# THE PAGE PRINTS THE POLICY IT WAS ACTUALLY SERVED, at the top, from its own
# response headers. If this block ever fails to apply, the probe says so in words
# instead of reporting a wall of failures that look like the other site refusing.
/probe.html
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self' blob: data: https:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https:; manifest-src 'self'; worker-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'
